03cspan class=\"mi\"\u003e0\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"p\"\u003e,\u003c/span\u003e \u003cspan class=\"mi\"\u003e0\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"p\"\u003e]\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"line\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"cl\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"n\"\u003eneigh\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"o\"\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"n\"\u003epredict\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"p\"\u003e([\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"n\"\u003etest_point\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"p\"\u003e])\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"line\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"cl\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"c1\"\u003e# array([1])\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eLet\u0026rsquo;s plot its 5 neighbors to figure out how KNN makes its decision. The black $\\times$ symbol represents the neighbors, and the red $\\times$ symbol is the new sample.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cfigure\u003e\u003cimg src=\"/img/KNN_classification.png\"\u003e\n\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFrom this diagram, we find that 4 neighbors have class label 1 and 1 neighbor has class label 0, so the KNN classifier predicts \u003ccode\u003e[0, 0]\u003c/code\u003e has class label 1.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 id=\"faq\" class=\"headerLink\"\u003e\n    \u003ca href=\"#faq\" class=\"header-mark\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eFAQ\u003c/h2\u003e\u003ch3 id=\"can-we-use-knn-on-the-unbalanced-dataset\" class=\"headerLink\"\u003e\n    \u003ca href=\"#can-we-use-knn-on-the-unbalanced-dataset\" class=\"header-mark\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eCan we use KNN on the unbalanced dataset?\u003c/h3\u003e\u003cp\u003eIt\u0026rsquo;s not recommended to use KNN in cases of unbalanced dataset, as the algorithm will \u003cem\u003ealmost always classify new samples into the majority class\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3 id=\"the-value-of-k-and-the-complexity-of-knn\" class=\"headerLink\"\u003e\n    \u003ca href=\"#the-value-of-k-and-the-complexity-of-knn\" class=\"header-mark\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eThe value of K and the complexity of KNN\u003c/h3\u003e\u003cp\u003eAn interesting question is: what\u0026rsquo;s the relationship between the value of $K$ and the complexity of KNN?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe answer is that the smaller the $K$ is, the more complex the model becomes, making it more prone to overfitting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eLooking at it the other way around: when $K$ is at its maximum, equal to the total number of samples ($N$), any new samples will always be classified as the majority class in the dataset. This kind of model is \u003cem\u003eobviously\u003c/em\u003e overly simplistic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3 id=\"should-we-normalize-the-sample-vector\" class=\"headerLink\"\u003e\n    \u003ca href=\"#should-we-normalize-the-sample-vector\" class=\"header-mark\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eShould we normalize the sample vector?\u003c/h3\u003e\u003cp\u003eYes. Because the KNN relies on the distance measurement. If the feature vector comes in vastly different scales, the feature with larger numerical values will dominate the distance calculation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3 id=\"can-we-use-knn-for-regression-problems\" class=\"headerLink\"\u003e\n    \u003ca href=\"#can-we-use-knn-for-regression-problems\" class=\"header-mark\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eCan we use KNN for regression problems?\u003c/h3\u003e\u003cp\u003eYes. In the classification settings, the KNN classifier uses a voting method to decide the class label. If we apply KNN to the regression problem, the prediction can be modeled as the weighted sum of $K$ neighbors\u0026rsquo;s value.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3 id=\"how-can-we-improve-the-naive-knn-algorithm\" class=\"headerLink\"\u003e\n    \u003ca href=\"#how-can-we-improve-the-naive-knn-algorithm\" class=\"header-mark\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eHow can we improve the naive KNN algorithm?\u003c/h3\u003e\u003cp\u003eA naive KNN algorithm uses voting to decide the class label of a new sample. In such a setting, each neighbor has the same contribution to the final prediction. One possible improvement may be to change the contribution of each label by its distance to a new sample. An intuition is: \u003cem\u003ethe closer a neighbor is, the more the neighbor contributes\u003c/em\u003e. A commonly used weighting schema is $1/d$ where $d$ is the distance to the new sample.\u003c/p\u003e\n",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "What is Phantom type in OCaml",
        "date_published" : "2024-12-08T16:33:18+08:00",
        "date_modified" : "2024-12-08T16:33:18+08:00",
        "id" : "https://martinlwx.github.io/en/what-is-phantom-type-in-ocaml/",
        "url" : "https://martinlwx.github.io/en/what-is-phantom-type-in-ocaml/",
        "summary": " Syntax Info The left side of = represents type, while the right side represents the value.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "MartinLwx"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003ch2 id=\"syntax\" class=\"headerLink\"\u003e\n    \u003ca href=\"#syntax\" class=\"header-mark\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eSyntax\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"details admonition info open\"\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class=\"details-summary admonition-title\"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class=\"icon\"\u003e\u003csvg class=\"icon\"\n    xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 512 512\"\u003e\u003c!-- Font Awesome Free 5.15.4 by @fontawesome - https://fontawesome.com License - https://fontawesome.com/license/free (Icons: CC BY 4.0, Fonts: SIL OFL 1.1, Code: MIT License) --\u003e\u003cpath d=\"M256 8C119.043 8 8 119.083 8 256c0 136.997 111.043 248 248 248s248-111.003 248-248C504 119.083 392.957 8 256 8zm0 110c23.196 0 42 18.804 42 42s-18.804 42-42 42-42-18.804-42-42 18.804-42 42-42zm56 254c0 6.627-5.373 12-12 12h-88c-6.627 0-12-5.373-12-12v-24c0-6.627 5.373-12 12-12h12v-64h-12c-6.627 0-12-5.373-12-12v-24c0-6.627 5.373-12 12-12h64c6.627 0 12 5.373 12 12v100h12c6.627 0 12 5.373 12 12v24z\"/\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e\u003c/span\u003eInfo\u003cspan class=\"details-icon\"\u003e\u003csvg class=\"icon\"\n    xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 256 512\"\u003e\u003c!-- Font Awesome Free 5.15.4 by @fontawesome - https://fontawesome.com License - https://fontawesome.com/license/free (Icons: CC BY 4.0, Fonts: SIL OFL 1.1, Code: MIT License) --\u003e\u003cpath d=\"M224.3 273l-136 136c-9.4 9.4-24.6 9.4-33.9 0l-22.6-22.6c-9.4-9.4-9.4-24.6 0-33.9l96.4-96.4-96.4-96.4c-9.4-9.4-9.4-24.6 0-33.9L54.3 103c9.4-9.4 24.6-9.4 33.9 0l136 136c9.5 9.4 9.5 24.6.1 34z\"/\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class=\"details-content\"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class=\"admonition-content\"\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe left side of \u003ccode\u003e=\u003c/code\u003e represents type, while the right side represents the value.\u003c/p\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cp\u003eTo use phantom type in OCaml, just add type parameters (such as \u003ccode\u003e'a\u003c/code\u003e) on the left side of \u003ccode\u003e=\u003c/code\u003e when defining a variant type, \u003cem\u003ebut do not use them on the right side\u003c/em\u003e. For example\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block highlight is-open show-line-numbers  tw:group tw:my-2\"\u003e\n  \u003cdiv class=\"\n    \n    tw:flex \n    tw:flex-row\n    tw:flex-1 \n    tw:justify-between \n    tw:w-full tw:bg-bgColor-secondary\n    \"\u003e      \n    \u003cbutton \n      class=\"\n        code-block-button\n        tw:mx-2 \n        tw:flex\n        tw:flex-row\n        tw:flex-1\"\n      aria-hidden=\"true\"\u003e\n          \u003cspan class=\"tw:group-[.is-open]:rotate-90 tw:transition-[transform] tw:duration-500 tw:ease-in-out tw:print:hidden! tw:w-min tw:h-min tw:my-1 tw:mx-1\"\u003e\u003csvg class=\"icon\"\n    xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 512\"\u003e\u003c!-- Font Awesome Free 5.15.4 by @fontawesome - https://fontawesome.com License - https://fontawesome.com/license/free (Icons: CC BY 4.0, Fonts: SIL OFL 1.1, Code: MIT License) --\u003e\u003cpath d=\"M285.476 272.971L91.132 467.314c-9.373 9.373-24.569 9.373-33.941 0l-22.667-22.667c-9.357-9.357-9.375-24.522-.04-33.901L188.505 256 34.484 101.255c-9.335-9.379-9.317-24.544.04-33.901l22.667-22.667c9.373-9.373 24.569-9.373 33.941 0L285.475 239.03c9.373 9.372 9.373 24.568.001 33.941z\"/\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\n          \u003cspan class=\"tw:select-none tw:my-1! tw:block\"\u003eocaml\u003c/span\u003e\n      \u003c/button\u003e\n\n   \u003cdiv class=\"tw:flex\"\u003e\n      \u003cbutton \n        class=\"\n          line-number-button\n          tw:mx-2 \n          tw:hidden \n          tw:group-[.is-open]:block \n          tw:group-[.show-line-numbers]:text-fgColor-link \n          tw:print:hidden!\" \n        title=\"Toggle line numbers\"\u003e\u003csvg class=\"icon\"\n    xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 512 5